In networking, classes are the categories that IP addresses were originally divided into. While modern networks mostly use CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) today, you'll still see the classes mentioned in cybersecurity, networking courses, and certification exams.

Class A
First octet: 1–126
Default subnet mask: 255.0.0.0 (/8)
Networks: Very few
Hosts per network: About 16.7 million

Example:

10.0.0.1
25.100.50.10

Used for: Very large organizations or ISPs.

Class B
First octet: 128–191
Default subnet mask: 255.255.0.0 (/16)
Hosts per network: About 65,534

Example:

172.16.5.10
150.25.100.1

Used for: Medium to large businesses.

Class C
First octet: 192–223
Default subnet mask: 255.255.255.0 (/24)
Hosts per network: 254

Example:

192.168.1.25
200.10.5.1

Used for: Small businesses and home networks.

Class D
First octet: 224–239

Purpose: Multicast

Instead of sending data to one computer, multicast sends it to a group of computers.

Example:

224.0.0.5

Used by routing protocols and streaming applications.

Class E
First octet: 240–255

Reserved for research and experimental purposes.

Normally, you won't assign these addresses to computers.

Easy Way to Remember
Class	First Number	Default Mask	Purpose
A	1–126	/8	Huge networks
B	128–191	/16	Medium networks
C	192–223	/24	Small networks
D	224–239	N/A	Multicast
E	240–255	N/A	Experimental
Private IP Ranges

These are used inside private networks and are not routable on the public internet.

Class	Private Range
A	10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255
B	172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255
C	192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255
What You Need to Know for Cybersecurity

For entry-level certifications and practical work, you should be able to:

Recognize an IP address's class by its first octet.
Know the common private IP ranges.
Understand that CIDR notation (such as /24, /16, and /8) has largely replaced classful networking because it allows much more flexible subnetting.
Recognize common examples like:
192.168.1.100 → Private Class C
10.1.2.3 → Private Class A
172.20.5.5 → Private Class B
8.8.8.8 → Public Class A
224.0.0.1 → Class D (multicast)
